  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 8: It only fails sometimes, and only on certain data"
            prompt="This chip's memory test mostly passes - most words check out clean - but a noticeable minority consistently come back wrong, and it isn't random: the same handful of addresses keep failing every run, while most others are completely solid. Write levelling looks fine (CK and DQS are well aligned as a byte lane). What training step is this, if it's not write levelling?"
            hint="Open the chip's editor and look at the per-bit DQ deskew indicators under each DQ column - is it every bit, evenly, or just one or two that stand out from an otherwise well-trained byte lane?"
            explanation="Write levelling (the whole-byte-lane DQS-to-CK alignment) is genuinely fine here - the symptom is narrower than that. Two individual DQ pins were left with real per-bit deskew error while the rest of the byte lane is correctly centred - the training step real DDR calls per-bit write-DQ deskew, centring each individual pin's data against DQS rather than the byte lane as a whole. Because only two of eight bits are actually wrong, a captured byte is only corrupted on the specific words where at least one of those two bits happens to differ from the previous word's same bit - which, for ordinary address-counting data, only happens on a minority of addresses. That's exactly why this looks &quot;intermittent&quot; and &quot;only on certain data&quot; rather than totally broken: it's a real, sparser failure signature, and it's the reason real DDR training per-bit deskews every single DQ pin individually rather than assuming one shared, byte-wide alignment (write levelling) is enough."/>
</LABCHAIN>
